>
  <Fragment slot="head">
  <style is:inline>
    /* A terminal frame is an artifact, not prose: column alignment IS the
       content, so the shared `.article-body pre` rule (white-space: pre-wrap;
       overflow-wrap: anywhere) must be turned off here. Without this the frame
       is silently garbled, worst at 320px, and no gate fails. A frame wider
       than the column scrolls inside its own box; the page never scrolls. */
    /* The selector must out-rank `.article-body pre`, not merely follow it.
       A bare `.tt-frame` is one class against one class plus one element, so
       the shared rule wins on specificity no matter what order the sheets are
       in — and the failure is silent: the frame wraps, every column shears,
       and the mobile gate goes GREEN because a wrapped frame cannot overflow.
       Measured that way once, at 320px, before this selector was qualified. */
    .article-body pre.tt-frame {
      white-space: pre;
      overflow-x: auto;
      overflow-wrap: normal;
      max-width: 100%;
    }
    /* `overflow-x: auto` LOOKS like it already prevents the page from
       scrolling sideways, and on this layout it does not. `main.wrap` is the
       breakout grid (ADR-008), so the figure is a grid item, and a grid item's
       default `min-width: auto` refuses to shrink below its content — an
       unwrappable 120-column frame therefore widens its own track and takes
       the page with it. Measured before this rule: 60px of horizontal overflow
       at 320px and 20px at 360px, on a page whose scroll box was already
       declared. Both the item and the box need an explicit zero. */
    .tt-figure,
    .article-body pre.tt-frame { min-width: 0; }

    /* Argument plate TT-1 (portfolio ADR-004, ADR-012). Page-scoped: the three
       states a gauge can be in, and the fact that only two of them are honest.
       Drawn from internal/hud/testdata/golden/zero-vs-absent.txt at 38f262a,
       which is the strongest artifact in that repository and CANNOT be pasted:
       it carries four distinct non-ASCII characters and has no ASCII twin, so
       a paste would fail the font-coverage gate. This plate carries the
       argument instead, and it carries no number that could go stale.
       State is word plus geometry first, colour second. Oxide marks the
       negative finding, which here is the invented reading.
       ADR-010 N/A: nothing here is interactive.
       ADR-014 moved the plate's scale and stroke ink to style.css: the figure
       ladder caps a plate at var(--fig-plate) and centers it, and every strong
       plate stroke reads var(--stroke-figure). At a 320px viewport the 272px
       track renders this 260-unit viewBox at scale 1.046, so a declared 9px
       stays at or above the 9px floor at every width. */
    .tt-plate .tt-track {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .tt-plate .tt-fill {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .tt-plate .tt-strike {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .tt-plate .tt-value {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 13px;
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .tt-plate .tt-value-absent {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 13px;
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .tt-plate .tt-value-bad {
      fill: var(--accent-warm);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 13px;
      font-weight: 600;
    }
    .tt-plate .tt-state {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    }
    .tt-plate .tt-state-absent { fill: var(--muted); }
    .tt-plate .tt-state-bad { fill: var(--accent-warm); }
    /* The microtext floor (CLAUDE.md, "Why 320px matters"). The class shipped
       at 8px and rendered at 8.00px at 320px, which is under the floor. The
       state rank above it moves 9px to 10px, so the two ranks stay apart.
       Scale note, re-measured 2026-08-31: the gallery ladder (ADR-014) now
       caps this plate at --fig-plate, which outranks the local 260px cap, so
       at 320px it renders 272px wide against a 260-unit viewBox: scale 1.046,
       and a declared 9px renders at 9.42px. The floor still clears, with room.
       Do not read "declared equals rendered" off this comment - it is no
       longer 1:1, and a plate below 1:1 owes a measurement. */
    .tt-plate .tt-micro {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .tt-plate-caption {
      color: var(--muted);
    }

    /* The two-slot caption CSS this island used to copy from
       context-architecture.astro now has ONE home in style.css (ADR-014,
       OC-F closed), together with the figure ladder's `min-width: 0`. This
       lane deleted the local copy per the landing order. */

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       `tt9-` IS THE SHARED GRAMMAR FOR THE FOUR NEW PLATES ON THIS PAGE.
       The prefix rule wants a digit, because the site's existing plate
       prefixes carry none and a duplicate marker id repaints the wrong arrow
       head with no gate to see it. One vocabulary for four plates on one page
       beats four copies of the same six rules.

       Rules these plates inherit, and a later editor must keep:
         - No digit in any <text> node. `figure-contract.cjs` fails on one, and
           every count lives in the caption or in kept prose.
         - Two hues only. `--accent-warm` means a decision that was taken back,
           so only the retired badge class uses it. Nothing else here is a
           reversal.
         - A state is a WORD plus a geometry. Geist has no glyph for the
           symbols that would replace them, and `false-green.astro` records
           that constraint for the whole site.
         - No `opacity` on text (ADR-009), and no italic: Geist ships no
           italic cut.
         - SVG text inherits from <body>, so every text class declares its own
           `font-family`.
         - The plate's scale and its strong stroke ink live in style.css now
           (ADR-014): the figure ladder caps a plate at var(--fig-plate) and
           centers it, and the strong strokes read var(--stroke-figure). The
           narrowest render is a 320px viewport, where the 272px track shows
           this 260-unit viewBox at scale 1.046, so the floor arithmetic below
           still holds at every width.
         - No text class declares under 9px. At scale >= 1.0 the declared size
           is the rendered floor, so 9px is the CLAUDE.md microtext floor.
       --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    .tt9-set,
    .tt9-band {
      fill: none;
      stroke-width: 1;
      stroke-dasharray: 3 3;
    }
    .tt9-box {
      fill: var(--accent-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .tt9-box-open {
      fill: none;
      stroke-width: 1;
      stroke-dasharray: 2 2;
    }
    .tt9-box-warm {
      fill: var(--accent-warm-soft);
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .tt9-panel {
      fill: none;
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .tt9-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent);
      stroke-width: 1.25;
    }
    /* A marker does not inherit the referencing path's stroke, so it declares
       its own paint. Without this the arrow head renders black in both themes. */
    .tt9-arrowhead { fill: var(--accent); stroke: none; }
    .tt9-strike {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--accent-warm);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    /* THE MICROTEXT FLOOR, and the three ranks that sit on it.
       CLAUDE.md rules the floor: no SVG text renders under 9px at a 320px
       viewport. Scale, re-measured 2026-08-31: the gallery ladder caps these
       plates at --fig-plate, which outranks their local 260px cap, so at
       320px each renders 272px against a 260-unit viewBox (scale 1.046). A
       declared size is therefore NOT the rendered size; multiply by 1.046.
       Measured at 320px before the fix: head 9.00px, label 8.00px, note
       7.50px. The label and the note were both under the floor.
       The note takes the floor, the label takes the floor, and the head moves
       to 10px so the head still outranks the label. Sans 700 against mono 400,
       and --text against --muted, carry the label-to-note rank instead of a
       size step. */
    .tt9-head {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 10px;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.07em;
    }
    .tt9-label {
      fill: var(--text);
      font-family: var(--font);
      font-size: 9px;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    }
    .tt9-note {
      fill: var(--muted);
      font-family: var(--mono);
      font-size: 9px;
    }
    .tt9-warm { fill: var(--accent-warm); }

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       STRIPS. A strip re-lays text nodes in a grid. It writes no new
       assertion, it keeps every word, and because it is HTML rather than SVG
       it stays inside `contrast-check.cjs`. `min-width: 0` on every grid item,
       or an unbreakable token widens its own track and takes the page with it.
       --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    .tt-strip { margin: 0; min-width: 0; }
    .tt-strip ul {
      list-style: none;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
    }
    .tt-strip li {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2px 18px;
      padding: 10px 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
      min-width: 0;
    }
    .tt-strip li > * { min-width: 0; }
    .tt-strip .tt-strip-a { color: var(--text); }
    .tt-strip .tt-strip-b { color: var(--muted); }
    /* `min-width: 0` on the header cells too, for the same reason the row cells
       get it: a grid item's default `min-width: auto` refuses to shrink below
       its content. Both header strings break on spaces today, so this is the
       rule the block already states rather than a fix for a live overflow. */
    .tt-strip-cols > * { min-width: 0; }
    .tt-strip-cols {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2px 18px;
      padding-bottom: 4px;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
    .tt-s2-group {
      margin: 16px 0 2px;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      font-weight: 700;
      letter-spacing: 0.06em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
    .tt-s2-group:first-of-type { margin-top: 4px; }
    /* The two-column split above 34rem lives in style.css now (ADR-014): the
       claim cell caps at minmax(0, 38rem) so the mechanic stays a readable
       line on the wide track. */
    /* tt-s2 groups a single-column list. It is a strip because it re-lays
       existing text under headings, not because it has two columns. The
       two-class selector out-ranks the media query at every width, so the
       order of these blocks cannot matter. */
    .tt-strip.tt-s2 li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .tt-strip.tt-s2 ul + p { margin-top: 18px; }
    /* tt-s3 is a TABLE, so it carries no `data-fig` and no caption slots: a
       table is not a figure (ADR-013) and it adds no unguarded claim. Its
       second column holds Go test names, which are single unbreakable tokens
       wider than the 320px content track. They must be allowed to break, or
       one identifier widens the page. */
    .tt-s3 code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

    /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       THE FRAME READER (portfolio ADR-013, RULE D2).

       EVERY FRAME SHIPS IN THE SERVED HTML AND JAVASCRIPT ONLY REDUCES THE
       SET. `scripts/mobile-qa.cjs` loads a page, measures the overflow once,
       and never clicks, so a frame swapped in after load is measured by
       nothing. The site's known silent failure is a `.tt-frame` that loses on
       specificity, wraps, shears every column, and turns the mobile gate GREEN
       because a wrapped frame cannot overflow. A later change that
       server-renders one frame reopens that hole in silence. Do not make it.

       The hiding rule is the ONLY thing the enhancement does, and it is
       written here rather than in the script so nothing flashes on load.
       --------------------------------------------------------------------- */
    .tt-reader[data-enhanced] figure[id^="frame-"]:not([data-current]) {
      display: none;
    }
    /* The reader's CHROME - the de-carded tab idiom, the ghost step buttons,
       the mono status, the phone scroll row, and the rhythm - lives in
       style.css now (ADR-014). This island keeps only what the page owns: the
       rail's structure, the 44px control boxes, and the focus ring. */
    .tt-reader-rail {
      list-style: none;
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      padding: 0;
    }
    .tt-reader-rail li { min-width: 0; }
    /* PL-9: at phone widths the seven labels ride ONE scrolling row.
       style.css declares the same flip, but this island loads after it and
       the base `flex-wrap: wrap` above would win the tie by source order.
       The island owns the rail's structure, so it flips its own declaration.
       The scroll box, the li flex basis, and the nowrap labels come from
       style.css. */
    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      .tt-reader-rail { flex-wrap: nowrap; }
    }
    /* A real control, not a word. 44px at every width, which is what the
       hit-target gate measures at phone widths for a standalone anchor. */
    .tt-reader-rail a,
    .tt-reader-step {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      min-height: 44px;
      padding: 0 12px;
      font-family: var(--mono);
    }
    .tt-reader-step { cursor: pointer; }
    .tt-reader-step:disabled { cursor: default; }
    .tt-reader-controls {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 10px;
    }
    .tt-reader-status {
      margin: 0;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
    .tt-reader figure:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 4px; }
  </style>
