    <h1>telltale</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">A terminal instrument for five coding-agent CLIs. It measures every reading, and a reading it cannot measure renders as absent, never as zero.</p>

    <div class="decision" aria-label="Case study summary">
      <p class="tag">Case study summary</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Result and evidence:</strong> v0.2.0 ships as four archives and a checksum file. Named tests enforce the honesty rules, not intent, so a reader can grep for them. The platform labels say which builds I ran and which I only built.</li>
        <li><strong>Problem:</strong> I direct five coding-agent CLIs across several repositories. None of them tells me how much context is left or what a run cost. None tells me whether its sandbox holds, or whether a seat that looks busy died quietly. Any dashboard I built would have to invent at least one of those numbers.</li>
        <li><strong>Ownership:</strong> Sole owner and technical lead. I set the honesty contract, the read-only boundary, and the release bar, and I made every ship-or-refuse call recorded on this page. Claude did most of the typing. The golden files and the postmortems are the proof.</li>
        <li><strong>Constraints:</strong> The gauge path reads; it never writes back to a vendor. The gauge path and the stdio agent mode bind no port and send nothing off the machine. Two modes are exceptions. The optional event listener binds a loopback port on 127.0.0.1 and refuses any other host. Council mode starts vendor CLIs, which talk to their vendors, and it can write to the workspace. Windows is the primary target. Every vendor changes its on-disk format, and no vendor announces it.</li>
        <li><strong>Actions:</strong> One Go binary. I built one adapter per vendor from its real records. I pinned the difference between zero and absent in three golden suites. I moved the README&rsquo;s pictures out of my hands and into the test suite. I raced one brief across five seats in isolated worktrees. I refused a list of features, and each one would have required a number nobody measured.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p class="lead">
      telltale is a Go terminal program. It reads what five coding-agent CLIs
      leave on disk. It renders one row per seat: context, spend, quota,
      liveness, and sandbox posture. The interesting part is not the gauge; it
      is the refusal. A gauge that fills a gap with a plausible number is worse
      than no gauge, because a reader trusts a plausible number. Most of the
      engineering here is the code that refuses to render.
    </p>

