        <rect class="tt9-box-open" x="150" y="50" width="16" height="14" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
        <rect class="tt9-box-open" x="170" y="50" width="16" height="14" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />

        <rect class="tt9-box-open" x="9" y="80" width="16" height="14" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
        <text class="tt9-label" x="31" y="89">DROP-FILE RELAY</text>
        <text class="tt9-note" x="31" y="101">MARKED SELF-REPORTED</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">
          telltale ships <span data-tt="adapters.vendor">7</span> vendor
          adapters, and the dispatch room seats five of them. The display
          watches the other two and does not seat them, on purpose. The
          drop-file relay is a sixth kind of row and it is not an adapter, so
          it sits outside the set.
        </span>
        <span class="fig-limit">
          The plate names no vendor, and it does not say which two the display
          only watches. The design document holds that list and the reason for
          it. The plate also does not show the relay format.
        </span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The design document says which two vendors the display only watches, and
      why. The relay format is documented and any tool can write it. So a
      program telltale ships no adapter for can still put a row on screen.
      telltale marks that row self-reported, because telltale did not measure
      it.
    </p>
    <p>
      An agent-facing mode serves the same document over stdio. It binds no
      port and it writes nothing. Install is a direct archive, a scoop manifest
      exercised once, or a source build.
      <a href="#frame-usage">See the display refuse a reading &rarr;</a>
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>What the release does and does not prove.</strong> Two of the four
      archives are built, not run: no recorded run exists for the macOS ARM
      build or the Linux build. All macOS measurement is one Intel machine. No
      binary is signed. Checksum verification against the release&rsquo;s own
      checksum file is therefore the whole verification the release offers. The
      Windows reputation prompt is unmeasured. One package-manager manifest set
      is a draft and I never submitted it.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="absent">Zero and absent are different states</h2>

    <!-- tt3-layers (portfolio ADR-013). The MECHANISM only: two independent
         places where an absence is enforced. `.tt-plate` above already argues
         the three render states, so this plate must not restate them. The three
         arguments in this section stay as prose: that a column of dashes is
         itself a claim, that a documented search is a different claim from no
         search, and the refusal to draw a context percentage. A plate cannot
         carry any of them at full strength. -->
    <figure class="fig" data-fig="plate">
      <span class="fig-label">Plate 03</span>
      <svg class="tt9-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 132" role="img"
           aria-label="Two independent layers enforce presence. Per session, every optional field is a pointer, so one field can drop on its own. Per adapter, a capability declaration drops a whole column and does not fill it with dashes.">
        <title>A pointer drops one field. A declaration drops a whole column.</title>

        <text class="tt9-head" x="2" y="10">TWO INDEPENDENT LAYERS</text>

        <rect class="tt9-band" x="1" y="16" width="258" height="48" rx="3" aria-hidden="true" />
        <text class="tt9-label" x="9" y="30">PER SESSION: A POINTER</text>
        <rect class="tt9-box" x="9" y="36" width="26" height="18" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
        <rect class="tt9-box" x="39" y="36" width="26" height="18" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
        <rect class="tt9-box-open" x="69" y="36" width="26" height="18" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
        <rect class="tt9-box" x="99" y="36" width="26" height="18" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
        <text class="tt9-note" x="136" y="49">ONE FIELD DROPS</text>

        <rect class="tt9-band" x="1" y="72" width="258" height="56" rx="3" aria-hidden="true" />
        <text class="tt9-label" x="9" y="86">PER ADAPTER: A DECLARATION</text>
        <rect class="tt9-box" x="9" y="92" width="26" height="28" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
        <rect class="tt9-box" x="39" y="92" width="26" height="28" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
        <rect class="tt9-box-open" x="69" y="92" width="26" height="28" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
        <text class="tt9-note" x="106" y="106">ONE COLUMN DROPS</text>
        <text class="tt9-note" x="106" y="118">DROPPED, NOT DASHED</text>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">
          An absence is enforced in two independent places, and neither one can
          cover for the other. A nil pointer on a session drops one field. A
          capability declaration on an adapter drops a whole column.
        </span>
        <span class="fig-limit">
          The plate does not show what a reader sees where the column used to
          be, and it shows no context percentage. Some vendors expose no
          per-model window, so that denominator does not exist.
        </span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Presence lives in two places, on purpose. Every optional field on a
      session is a pointer, so an absent reading is a nil and never a sentinel
      number. A rendered <code>0</code> therefore always means the vendor said
      zero. Above that sits a capability declaration per adapter: a vendor that
      can never supply a field declares it unsupported. A column of dashes for
      a vendor that could never fill it is itself a small claim about what was
      measured.
    </p>
    <p>
      The strongest detail in the repository is the way the code documents one
      of those declarations. The code does not merely state it. The package
      documentation names the fields the survey looked for in a live corpus,
      and it records that the search returned no matches. An absence documented
      with the search that found nothing is a different claim from an absence
      nobody looked for.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same rule is why there is no context percentage for some vendors. A
      percentage needs a per-model window as its denominator. Where the vendor
      does not expose one, any percentage would be arithmetic on a number I
      made up.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span data-tt="goldens.zeroVsAbsent">3</span> golden files, in three
      different packages, fail the build when a measured zero and an absence
      ever render the same. A stored render enforces the rule, not intent,
      because intent does not fail a build.
    </p>
    <p class="tradeoff">
      <strong>The rule:</strong> a gauge may state a number, or it may state
      nothing. It may not state a number it did not measure. An absence
      documented with the search that found nothing is a different claim from
      an absence nobody looked for. That difference is the whole product.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="badges">The badges claimed more than I measured</h2>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>A badge with no evidence class</p>
      <h3>The row read like enforcement, and one seat had a mechanism</h3>
      <p>
        Each seat in the room carries a badge that describes its sandbox
        posture. The first version of that row read as enforcement across four
        seats. Exactly one of the four had a measured mechanism behind it.
        Nobody wrote a false sentence. The badge simply did not say what kind
        of thing it was.
      </p>

      <!-- tt5-badges (portfolio ADR-013). The four cells, and the struck cell
           reuses the INVENTED geometry from `.tt-plate` above so the page has
           one vocabulary for "this one is the failure". `--accent-warm` is
           correct here and nowhere else on this page: the retirement is a
           decision that was taken back.
           NO VENDOR NAME ENTERS THIS PLATE, ITS TITLE, ITS CAPTION, OR ITS
           `aria-label`. The measured two-direction example below stays as
           prose with no identifying shape, and there is no per-vendor verdict
           table anywhere on this site.

           THE FOUR CELLS WRAP TO TWO ROWS, and the microtext floor is the
           reason. Four cells across 260 units gave each cell 62 units. At the
           9px floor the widest line, "IT READ LIKE", measures 65.4 units. A
           four-across row cannot hold it. Four cells plus their gaps need about
           261 units, and the plate has 258. The reading order is unchanged,
           because a wrap reads left to right and then down. The cost is height:
           the plate grows from 96 units to 152. -->
      <figure class="fig" data-fig="plate">
        <span class="fig-label">Plate 04</span>
        <svg class="tt9-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 152" role="img"
             aria-label="Four badge classes. Enforced: a live run measured the restriction holding. Refuted: a live run measured it failing. Unverified: I looked for a result and got none. Claimed: retired, and struck through, because it read like enforced and was not.">
          <title>Three evidence classes are live, and the fourth is retired.</title>

          <text class="tt9-head" x="2" y="10">EVERY BADGE NAMES ITS EVIDENCE CLASS</text>

          <rect class="tt9-panel" x="1" y="18" width="128" height="64" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
          <text class="tt9-label" x="65" y="36" text-anchor="middle">ENFORCED</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="65" y="52" text-anchor="middle">A LIVE RUN</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="65" y="64" text-anchor="middle">MEASURED IT</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="65" y="76" text-anchor="middle">HOLDING</text>

          <rect class="tt9-panel" x="131" y="18" width="128" height="64" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
          <text class="tt9-label" x="195" y="36" text-anchor="middle">REFUTED</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="195" y="52" text-anchor="middle">A LIVE RUN</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="195" y="64" text-anchor="middle">MEASURED IT</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="195" y="76" text-anchor="middle">FAILING</text>

          <rect class="tt9-panel" x="1" y="86" width="128" height="64" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
          <text class="tt9-label" x="65" y="104" text-anchor="middle">UNVERIFIED</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="65" y="120" text-anchor="middle">LOOKED FOR,</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="65" y="132" text-anchor="middle">NO RESULT</text>

          <rect class="tt9-panel" x="131" y="86" width="128" height="64" rx="2" aria-hidden="true" />
          <text class="tt9-label tt9-warm" x="195" y="104" text-anchor="middle">CLAIMED</text>
          <path class="tt9-strike" d="M 169 100 L 221 100" aria-hidden="true" />
          <text class="tt9-note tt9-warm" x="195" y="120" text-anchor="middle">RETIRED</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="195" y="132" text-anchor="middle">IT READ LIKE</text>
          <text class="tt9-note" x="195" y="144" text-anchor="middle">ENFORCEMENT</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          <span class="fig-what">
            A badge names its evidence class, so the reader is not left to
            supply the strongest reading. Three classes are live: enforced,
            refuted, and unverified. The fourth class, claimed, is retired.
          </span>
          <span class="fig-limit">
            The plate names no vendor and no seat. It shows no per-vendor
            sandbox posture and no verdict table, because which settings do not
            contain writes is a bypass instruction.
          </span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The fix was not a better badge. Every badge now names its evidence
        class, and there are three: <strong>enforced</strong>,
        <strong>refuted</strong>, and <strong>unverified</strong>. I retired a
        fourth class, because &ldquo;claimed&rdquo; read like
        &ldquo;enforced&rdquo; and was not. The original error stays in the
        design document as the reason the rule exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        One seat moved in both directions, and that is the only way to show the
        classes are real. It carried a weaker badge, because at one tool version
        a read-only flag failed every process spawn. It earned the stronger
        badge back on a later re-probe. That re-probe measured a live write
        denied, with no file left on disk.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do not name the seat, and I do not publish a per-vendor verdict table.
        Which settings do not contain writes is a bypass instruction. A negative
        finding measured once by one operator is not a fact about
        somebody&rsquo;s product.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same vocabulary covers the seats I considered and did not take, so
        &ldquo;I looked and said no&rdquo; never reads like &ldquo;nobody
        looked&rdquo;. The record holds every non-seated candidate with its
        evidence class. It records one as installed, waitlisted, and explicitly
        not a rejection.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The lesson:</strong> a badge that does not name its evidence
        class is a rumour with a border. A reader supplies the strongest
        reading available. So the claim has to carry its own evidence class, or
        it lies by default.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="race">Five seats, one brief, and no winner</h2>
    <p>
      telltale races one brief across every seat at once. Each attempt runs in
      its own git worktree. A diff compares the attempts, and prose does not.
      Two of the mechanics below exist only to stop a lying diff.
    </p>

    <!-- tt-s1 (portfolio ADR-013). A STRIP re-lays existing text nodes in a
         grid. It writes no new assertion, every cell keeps the words the prose
         used, and because it is HTML rather than SVG it stays inside
         `contrast-check.cjs`.
         SIX ROWS, NOT SEVEN, AND THE COUNT IS A PARITY DECISION. The prose
         names the failure it blocks for six mechanics. It does not name one for
         the undecided-rate rule, so that rule stays as prose below rather than
         read a blank cell or an invented failure. A blank cell begs to be
         filled, and filling it inflates the figure past its own prose.
         The two rulings stay as prose for the same reason: that an undercount
         is the honest direction to be wrong in, and that one race is not a
         benchmark. A strip cannot carry a ruling at full strength. -->
    <figure class="fig" data-fig="strip">
      <span class="fig-label">Strip 05</span>
      <div class="tt-strip">
        <!-- NOT `aria-hidden`. These two are column LABELS, and CLAUDE.md's
             contrast rule names exactly this case: marking something
             aria-hidden asserts it is decoration that nobody reads, and a
             label is not decoration. A screen reader should hear the pairing
             the sighted reader sees. -->
        <div class="tt-strip-cols">
          <span>The mechanic</span>
          <span>The failure it blocks</span>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <span class="tt-strip-a">The diff anchors on a recorded base commit and never on <code>HEAD</code>.</span>
            <span class="tt-strip-b">A lying diff.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tt-strip-a">Untracked files are staged first.</span>
            <span class="tt-strip-b">An attempt whose entire answer is a new file reads as &ldquo;no changes&rdquo;.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tt-strip-a">A check grades an attempt from the real exit code and nothing else.</span>
            <span class="tt-strip-b">Not from output, not from the diff, not from duration, and not from a model&rsquo;s opinion.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tt-strip-a">A command that could not run reads &ldquo;check unavailable&rdquo;.</span>
            <span class="tt-strip-b">It is not a FAIL.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tt-strip-a">The standings say <strong>adopted</strong>, never <strong>won</strong>.</span>
            <span class="tt-strip-b">The git refs can record that a branch merged. They cannot record a rank, a phase, or that a seat was cut.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="tt-strip-a">Hand-cut receipts are refused outright.</span>
            <span class="tt-strip-b">The figure is an undercount.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        <span class="fig-what">
          Six race mechanics, each beside the failure the page says it blocks.
          Every cell keeps the words the prose used, and the two column names
          are the only new words.
        </span>
        <span class="fig-limit">
          The strip names no seat and reports no result. One race is not a
          benchmark, so there is no ranking to show. It also leaves out the
          undecided-rate rule, because the prose never says what that rule
          blocks.
        </span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The standings report an undecided race beside the rate and never inside
      it, and the rate never prints without its count. An undercount is the
      honest direction to be wrong in.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each finisher carries a rank, a phase word, its own clock, and a commit
      receipt. A seat that I cut by hand keeps its receipt too. The first race
      seated all five seats. The first finish came in at
      <span data-tt="race.firstFinish">50s</span>, and two more finished within
      about twenty seconds of it. I gave up two seats by hand after roughly
      <span data-tt="race.givenUp">11 minutes</span>. Both seats kept their
      commits, exactly as the finish-line design promised.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page cites those two figures from the design document&rsquo;s section
      on the race. It does not re-measure them.
      <a href="#frame-record">See the record state its reach &rarr;</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>I withhold the seat names, and the reason is the point.</strong>
      One race is not a benchmark. A ranking published off a single brief on a
      single machine would be a claim about five products. The measurement
      cannot support that claim. The standings inside the program refuse the
      same thing for the same reason.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>What has no live half yet.</strong> No live race ever ran under a
      check, and I never armed a real hybrid adopt. Both are built and both are
      unexercised.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="instrument">Twice the instrument was the bug</h2>
    <p>
      Four corrections, and two of them are the same shape. The thing that was
      wrong was my own measuring equipment. It failed in one direction: it
      reported nothing found.
    </p>

    <!-- tt4-sweep (portfolio ADR-013). The verdict word is INSTRUMENT, not
         DRIFT, and that is the whole point of the plate: an old session with
         the identical key set is what rules out the vendor. The two figures are
         `cited`, so they stay in the prose below with their design-document
         reference. A plate cannot carry a citation, and a figure that drops a
         citation is worse than no figure. -->
    <figure class="fig" data-fig="plate">
      <span class="fig-label">Plate 06</span>
      <svg class="tt9-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 118" role="img"
           aria-label="The record on disk holds the keys. The sweep sits between the record and the answer, and its pattern matched almost nothing, so it reported nothing found. A session recorded weeks earlier holds the identical key set. The verdict is instrument, not drift.">
        <title>An old session with the identical key set rules out vendor drift.</title>
        <defs>
          <!-- The prefix carries a digit, because the site's existing marker
               prefixes carry none and a duplicate id repaints the wrong arrow
               head with no gate to see it. A marker does not inherit the
               referencing path's stroke, so `.tt9-arrowhead` sets its own. -->
          <marker id="tt9-arrow" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4"
                  markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto">
            <path class="tt9-arrowhead" d="M 0 0 L 8 4 L 0 8 z" />
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <text class="tt9-head" x="2" y="10">WHAT THE SWEEP COULD SEE</text>

