      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>The regex answered the question it was asked</p>
      <h3>A sweep reported one key, and the record carried eleven</h3>
      <p>
        A pattern sweep behind one vendor&rsquo;s cost row concluded that the
        vendor exposed a cost total and nothing else. I anchored the pattern on
        the substring <code>cost</code>, and I wrote it in lowercase. A key
        spelled with a capital letter in the middle matched neither half. The
        pattern was structurally incapable of the question a reader takes that
        row to answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        A live re-measure read <span data-tt="sweep.keysPresent">eleven keys</span>
        off the same turn record. The sweep could match
        <span data-tt="sweep.keysMatched">one key</span> and reported one key.
        A session recorded weeks earlier carried the identical key set, and that
        settles it: this was never vendor drift. The counts were on disk before
        I wrote the adapter. This page cites both figures from the design
        document&rsquo;s own correction block.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same class of mistake landed a second time. A pattern swept file
        contents, and it could never match a directory name.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The rule this bought:</strong> before I build a vendor here,
        re-read its records, not its struct. A record struct is an allowlist,
        so what it omits is a decision and not an absence. Both instrument bugs
        failed in the same direction: they reported nothing found. That is the
        one failure mode this product is least able to see in itself.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">A green test over the wrong prompt</p>
      <h3>A live test stayed green while one seat was dead for a day</h3>
      <p>
        I separated one seat&rsquo;s command-line flag from its value. That
        vendor&rsquo;s parser refuses a brief that opens with three dashes.
        Every briefed turn died with an empty column. The live test stayed
        green the whole time, because it probed with a prompt that began with a
        letter.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The lesson:</strong> probe a vendor with a prompt shaped like a
        brief, not like a greeting. I brief-fence every later probe for that
        reason.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">A guard that read a live process as health</p>
      <h3>A finished racer read as streaming for twenty-one minutes</h3>
      <p>
        A stale-exit guard attributed a live process to the wrong seat. A racer
        that already exited therefore left its column live for
        <span data-tt="stall.streaming">21 minutes</span> after the fact: no
        diff, no commit, no rank, no receipt. Two earlier drives wrote it off as
        a vendor that quit. It was not the vendor.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fix was attribution, and I checked it the same day. The column
        retired in <span data-tt="stall.retired">18 seconds</span>. This page
        cites both durations from the design document&rsquo;s measurement block.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>The lesson:</strong> a debt that will not pay may be a broken
        feature rather than an unfinished errand.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>Pictures a human maintained</p>
      <h3>The README&rsquo;s frames drifted, and one of them showed a number the code refuses to compute</h3>
      <p>
        I drew the frames in the README by hand. They drifted several ways at
        once. One drift was a cost line that showed a derived figure the
        codebase deliberately does not compute. A picture of a program that the
        program did not render is a claim with no check on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The test suite now emits them from the real render, and a test fails the
        build when they stop matching.
      </p>
      <p class="tradeoff">
        <strong>Why it governs this page too:</strong> a picture a human
        maintains is a claim with no gate on it. A script re-read every count on
        this page from the repository at build time. The page prints the commit
        it read them at under Stack.
      </p>
    </div>

