    <h2 id="refusals">What it refuses to render</h2>
    <p>
      I considered each of these, I argued it, and I declined it. Each refusal
      is a file in the repository with the argument still attached.
    </p>
    <ul class="ledger">
      <li><strong>No model judges a check.</strong> An estimate marker means a figure the program computed, and an opinion is not a computation.</li>
      <li><strong>No rating or ranking on the standings.</strong> A rating is a number with no measurement under it.</li>
      <li><strong>No fleet total, anywhere.</strong> Each run reports one vendor, so the arithmetic is not available to make. A test fails the build when a total appears.</li>
      <li><strong>No &ldquo;percent of plan&rdquo; spend meter.</strong> The plan budget is a guess.</li>
      <li><strong>No cost estimated from a price table.</strong> That invents money out of token counts.</li>
      <li><strong>No cross-device sync.</strong> Network egress breaks the no-write contract.</li>
      <li><strong>No plugin runtime for third-party adapters.</strong> It would run a stranger&rsquo;s code inside the process whose entire contract is &ldquo;reads, never writes&rdquo;. A drop-file relay is the middle path I took instead.</li>
      <li><strong>No pre-seeded rows in a table.</strong> A rectangular table is a table of claims, and every zero cell would state a request nobody sent.</li>
      <li><strong>No star count, download count, install count, or &ldquo;used by&rdquo; badge.</strong> The program measures none of them, and a third-party render of an unmeasured number is the badge form of a rendered guess.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The review keys are the same rule in a smaller place. A press lands a
      quoted hunk in the live composer draft, and nothing else happens. The
      surveyed alternatives route a comment straight back to the agent. This
      room cannot do that honestly, because a race attempt is one-shot and it is
      finished before you read it. A person who presses a key stays the only
      thing that spends a quota. A test asserts that: it counts the processes
      spawned and the turns started, and it does not check the wording.
      <a href="#frame-gate">See the gate bar &rarr;</a>
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="capacity">The gate that measured the capacity</h2>
    <p>
      I pointed the same habit that gates a model choice on this site at the
      build capacity itself. On a fixed date I wrote down six evidence axes and
      a close date. I froze every change until the close, and I let the axes
      decide. The month returned a step <strong>down</strong>. It also returned
      a process finding I did not want: nothing ever wrote to the axes. I tell
      that one on the program page, because it is a program artifact and not a
      product feature.
      <a href="product-and-program.html#capacity-gate">The capacity gate &rarr;</a>
    </p>

