    <h2 id="stack">Stack</h2>
    <p>
      Go, one static binary, no runtime dependency. The program binds no network
      listener by default. The agent-facing mode serves the same document over
      stdio and binds no port. No telemetry, no price table, and no model call
      anywhere in the gauge path. Public repository:
      <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/telltale">github.com/sanlee-ys/telltale</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The honesty rules are named tests, so a skeptic can grep rather than
      trust:
    </p>

    <!-- tt-s3. A TABLE, not a figure: it carries existing text, adds no
         unguarded claim, and therefore takes no `data-fig` and no caption
         slots (ADR-013). The `TestMain` row carries only what the test fails
         the build on. The real-money incident is an argument about a
         measurement, so it stays as prose below the table and never inside a
         cell. -->
    <table class="tt-s3">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">The honesty rule</th>
          <th scope="col">The test that fails the build</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>A measured zero and an absence never render the same.</td>
          <td>the three <code>zero-vs-absent</code> goldens, in three packages, read by <code>TestGoldenZeroVsAbsent</code>, <code>TestGoldenRenders</code>, and <code>TestGoldenDocuments</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>No total is rendered anywhere. The assertion reads the rendered string, not the code.</td>
          <td><code>TestNoTotalIsRenderedAnywhere</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>No gauge package can reach the verbatim event store.</td>
          <td><code>TestNoGaugeReadsTheEventStore</code>, which walks the transitive import graph</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A review key seeds the draft and spends no quota.</td>
          <td><code>TestDQuotesIntoTheDraftAndSpawnsNothing</code>, which counts processes and turns</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>No test reaches a real vendor binary.</td>
          <td>the council package&rsquo;s <code>TestMain</code>, which <strong>panics</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A quota window never renders without its rate-limit source.</td>
          <td>a CI step that pipes a fixture through the <strong>built binary</strong> and greps its own standard output</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      <code>TestMain</code>&rsquo;s default used to be the opposite. I measured
      that default, and it spent real money. A plain suite run on a machine with
      a vendor installed opened a live, write-enabled turn on my own account. CI
      could never find that, because CI has no vendors installed.
    </p>
    <p>
      I do not print the permission-bypass flag name, the per-vendor sandbox
      settings, or a containment table. Any of those names publishes a bypass
      instruction.
    </p>
